---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import litigation from '../data/litigation.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const t = [
  ['11 Mar 1941', 'Henrique born', 'Ivone is seventeen. The father is a Pinto da Rocha. The son stays behind when she goes south.', ''],
  ['2 Feb 1948', 'Ivone marries de Witt', 'At Germiston. He is 24, born in Johannesburg in December 1923.', 'contradiction'],
  ['19 Jun 1948', 'Ivone marries Kurt Luwinski', 'At Johannesburg. Certificate 858. She is entered as Ivone de Figueiredo de Witt, aged 24. He is 36.', 'key'],
  ['after Jun 1948', 'Kurt files for divorce', 'The tree records it as an attempted divorce filed, citing the South African national archives. Weeks, at most, after the wedding.', 'key'],
  ['1951', 'Divorce granted', 'Illiquid case. Restitution of conjugal rights. Kurt Luwinski versus Ivone, formerly de Witt, born Figueiredo.', 'key'],
  ['13 Feb 1953', 'Ivone marries Morris Salkow', 'Entered as Ivone Luwinski.', ''],
  ['29 Feb 1956', 'Kurt marries Frances Hostowsky', 'Registered in the Hanover, Orange Free State records.', ''],
  ['1957', 'Kurt files for divorce again', 'Versus Frances, formerly Sefton, formerly Berner, born Hostowsky.', ''],
  ['Nov 1962', 'Henrique reaches Johannesburg', 'Twenty-one years after his birth, and fourteen after his mother married Kurt.', ''],
  ['15 Dec 1968', 'Kurt dies at Johannesburg', 'Buried at Westpark the next day, block L grave 211. Estate file 7584/1969 follows.', ''],
];
---
<Base title="1948"
  subtitle="Two marriages, one divorce action, and a child"
  lede="Set the dates in a column and the year stops looking like a marriage and starts looking like something else. None of this was noticed until the DNA gave a reason to look.">

  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Event</th><th></th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {t.map(([d, e, n, k]) => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{d}</strong></td>
          <td>{e}{k === 'contradiction' && <> <span class="tag disp">Contradiction</span></>}{k === 'inferred' && <> <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span></>}</td>
          <td class="small muted">{n}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Six actions in nine years</h2>
  <p>
    All nine images attached to Ivone and Kurt in the family tree have now been opened.
    <strong>None is a photograph of a face.</strong> Two are scans of marriage registers; the rest
    are photographs of a NAAIRS screen — somebody's research session, captured, filed as “photos”
    and never read again. <strong>Three of the court records in them were not in this archive.</strong>
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Year</th><th>Who sued whom</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{litigation.map(r => (
      <tr>
        <td><strong>{r.ref}</strong></td>
        <td>{r.year}</td>
        <td>{r.plaintiff} → {r.defendant}</td>
        <td>{r.action}{r.status.startsWith('NEW') && <> <span class="tag doc">New</span></>}</td>
      </tr>
    ))}</tbody>
  </table></div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The four-month gap is explained <span class="tag doc">Solved</span></h3>
    <p>
      This archive could not see how Ivone was a <strong>spinster</strong> on 2 February 1948 and
      <strong>divorced</strong> on 19 June. <strong>TPD 534/1948 is the answer: her husband sued
      her.</strong> <em>Johannes Antonie Oelofse de Witt versus Yvonne (born de Figueiredo)</em>,
      divorce, brought in the Transvaal Provincial Division and evidently undefended — which is how
      a marriage of four months was over in four months.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      She married a miner in February, he divorced her by June, and she married Kurt seventeen days
      after that.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And she was not only the defendant <span class="tag doc">New</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>WLD 2545/1950 — <em>Ivone Luwenski (formerly de Witt, born Figueiredo) versus
      Kurt</em>.</strong> She brought it. Restitution of conjugal rights is the action a
      <strong>deserted</strong> spouse brings to compel the other to return, and in South African
      practice it was the necessary first step before a divorce for desertion.
    </p>
    <p>
      So this is not one man disposing of a wife:
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      <strong>1948</strong> — he files to end it, weeks after the wedding, before the child is born.<br />
      <strong>1950</strong> — <strong>she</strong> files, to make him come back.<br />
      <strong>1951</strong> — he files again, twice, and this time it ends.
    </blockquote>
    <p class="small">Three years, three actions, one child. <strong>Whatever else it was, she fought it.</strong></p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Read a third time, from the film itself <span class="tag doc">Verified</span></h3>
    <p>
      The 1948 register had been read twice, both times from a <strong>photograph attached to the
      family tree</strong> — a copy of a copy. This archive has already been caught once treating a
      transcription as a document, so on 15 September it was fetched from the microfilm:
      <strong>digital film 008109682, frame 2047</strong>, in <em>South Africa, Civil Marriage
      Records, 1801–1974</em>. FamilySearch's own pages would not render — the session had been
      rate-limited to about one response every thirty-five seconds, which the site's front end shows
      as a hang — so the page was rebuilt locally from its <strong>108 deep-zoom tiles</strong> and
      every cell magnified four to five times, with the sideways columns rotated upright.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>One correction, and it is a real one.</strong> The address this archive published —
      <s>108 Dunstan Street, Braamfontein</s> — is wrong. The film reads
      <strong>108 Dunottar Street, Sydenham, Johannesburg</strong>. Rotated upright at 4.7× the two
      are not close: <em>Dunottar</em> carries the double <em>t</em> of the Scottish castle name, and
      the second line is a short word beginning <em>Syd</em> where <em>Braamfontein</em> is long and
      begins <em>Br</em>. Dunottar Street, Sydenham 2192 is a real street and still is. Braamfontein
      is the city end of town; <strong>Sydenham in 1948 was one of Johannesburg's Jewish northern
      suburbs</strong>. It has been corrected in five places.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Everything the argument rests on holds.</strong> Checked cell by cell: <em>858/1948</em>;
      Kurt, 36, <em>Berlin, Germany</em>, <strong>Bachelor</strong>, shop-keeper; Ivone, 24,
      <em>Portuguese East Africa</em>, <strong>Divorcee</strong>, supervisor; special licence
      <strong>164531</strong> of 9 June; and column (10) — read at 5.2×, where the word is four
      letters — <strong>With A.N.C.</strong>, not <em>Without</em>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Two things the tree copies did not carry <span class="tag doc">New</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>It was a magistrate's wedding, and nobody was told.</strong> The sheet is stamped
      <strong>Magistrate's Office, Johannesburg</strong> twice, and the two witnesses —
      <em>E. W. Coetzee</em> and <em>P. H. Els</em> — are the office's own people, not family or
      friends. A special licence taken out ten days earlier means <strong>no banns were called</strong>:
      nothing was announced anywhere. Two strangers off the corridor signed for them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>“Order Filed” is a physical document, in a second place.</strong> A divorcée could not
      remarry in 1948 without producing the decree, so the remark means the order in
      <strong>TPD 534/1948</strong> was handed in on the day and <strong>filed with Johannesburg
      entry 858</strong>. The court file is already on the Pretoria request; this says a copy also sits
      behind the marriage entry at Home Affairs. Two custodies for one document that nobody has read.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>She aged two years in four months <span class="tag disp">Contradiction</span></h3>
    <p>
      Germiston, 2 February 1948: <strong>22</strong>. Johannesburg, 19 June 1948: <strong>24</strong>.
      Both numbers are on official registers she signed, four and a half months apart. At least one is
      wrong. If the June age is the true one she was born in 1924, which is what everything else in
      this archive says — and the February <em>22</em> is then either a woman taking two years off or a
      clerk guessing.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      It changes nothing in the argument. It is recorded so that nobody finds it again in a year's time
      and thinks it is new.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What is odd about it</h2>
  <p>
    <s><strong>The gap.</strong> Four and a half months with no divorce recorded in between, so one
    of the dates must be wrong.</s> <strong>Both dates were right and the divorce existed.</strong>
    It is TPD 534/1948, above — found in a photograph in the family tree.
    <a href={base + '/ivone/'}>Ivone's page →</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The filing.</strong> Kurt files for divorce within weeks of his own wedding, and before his
    wife gives birth. That is not a marriage that wore out. Something was wrong with it almost at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The timing of the birth.</strong> The child was conceived after the wedding, not before
    it. On its face that is unremarkable. It only becomes remarkable alongside
    a DNA result showing <a href={base + '/dna/'}>no trace of Kurt's father or Kurt's mother</a> in the
    child.
  </p>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>A warning about reading backwards <span class="tag inf">Method</span></h3>
    <p>
      Everything on this page was in the record before the DNA was looked at, and none of it was thought
      to mean anything. That should make anyone careful. A timeline read in the light of a conclusion
      will always seem to support it.
    </p>
    <p>
      So: the dates here are documented and the inference is labelled as an inference, and they are kept
      apart on purpose. If the Y-DNA came back Ashkenazi, this page would stay exactly as it is and the
      conclusion would go — a marriage in trouble from the first month, and nothing more.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>It came back on 14 September 2026, and it is not Ashkenazi.</strong> Derrick's paternal
      haplogroup is <strong>R-S11477</strong> — R1b, branch U106/Z381, the North Sea lineage that peaks
      in the Netherlands and England. The page still stands on its documents and the conclusion still
      stands apart from them, exactly as it did before. But the test this page named as the one that
      could take the conclusion away has been run, and it did not take it away.
      <a href={base + '/dna/'}>What it proves and what it doesn't →</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What sits behind it</h2>
  <p>
    Kurt was thirty-six, fifteen years out of Germany, and had five years earlier lost his father to a
    transport from Berlin he can only have learned about second-hand and late. Ivone was
    twenty-four, born in Portuguese East Africa, with a seven-year-old son in another country. They
    married in June, he sued in the winter, the child came in the autumn, and the court took until 1951
    to let them go. Neither of them was in the country either had been born in.
  </p>
</Base>
